Wing: Flat high wing with winglets
An airplane generates lift through Bernoulli's Principle and Newton's Third Law, using wing curvature and air deflection to create an upward pressure difference.
Without sustained thrust during a glide, an aircraft balances its weight against lift and drag by angling slightly downward, converting altitude into forward momentum.
Unlike high-speed, heavy-winged conventional darts, advanced gliders feature a large wing area and low wing loading, allowing them to float efficiently at slower speeds while maintaining stable flight via a forward centre of gravity.

